ues are decorated with flowers and [UNCLEAR] had no fresh flowers, but he had paper flowers, and he gave the Jews the paper flowers to decorate the synagogue. And one other thing is interesting. The habit of [UNCLEAR] Jewish men is to keep their prayer books lying on a chair when the prayers are finished. He designated a captain-- a sailor, to pick up all these prayer books and to stack them neatly on a table in the synagogue, which is quite amazing. A German, maybe even a Nazi, taking the Jewish books and stacking them nice and neatly on the table. And when the ship got to Cuba, the Cuban government would not recognize the landing pass because the landing pass actually had been sold by an official of the Cuban government and not by the Cuban government. And the Cuban government would not recognize it and would not let anybody off the ship, except if you had family already in Cuba. [PAUSES] The story continues that after a while, the Cuban government sent the ship out of Havana harbor because it was taking up too much space. And the ship, the St. Louis, went up and down the Florida coast. And, you know, Cuba is not far away from from Florida. And at night, the Jewish passengers on board the ship could see the lights of Miami Beach. But nobody dared jump off the ship because both the United States Coast Guard and the Cuban Coast Guard were circling the boats all the time to make sure that nobody jumped off the ship, and they went all the way up to Canada. And [UNCLEAR] was a-- Jewish organizations worked with the United States government, and the Canadian government, and the Dominion government and many other governments to try to find a way, to find a home for the Jews on board. But nobody was willing to take the Jews, which naturally was just what Hitler wanted. He said, "If your countries won't take the Jews, I don't want the Jews either." And that's when he decided to send all the Jews to concentration camps. And after a week of going up and down the Florida coast, the Captain realized he was running out of food and he radioed his headquarters in Hamburg and he was told, come back to Hamburg. And the Jews, when they were told what was happening, they were are going back to Germany, realizing that going back to Germany meant going directly to the concentration camp, because they had notified the police that they were giving up their homes, so they had no homes to go back to. And going back to Germany means going back to concentration camp. And the Jewish organizations worked with the European countries trying to find a way to a solution. And just three days before the ship was supposed to arrive in Hamburg, four countries, England, France, Holland and Belgium, each one took a part of the Jews, so that none of the Jews had to go back to Germany. My father was landed in Holland and he was put into a transit camp, and he immediately notified the American consulate that here I am, I'm waiting for my visa. [PAUSES 8 SECONDS]

