 bar mitzvah was an affair not like it used to be during peacetime. Nobody had any money to buy gifts. If you wanted to buy a gift, you had to make it yourself. And the gift that I cherished most was a bookmark that my teacher made for me. [CLEARS THROAT] [PAUSES] With a quotation from the Tehillim, one of the Jewish books, and my name and my birth date, and his name and his signature. And he gave it to me. That was my which I brought also to the United States and donated to the museum. [DRINKING]

Although the services for the bar mitzvah were normal, there was no rabbi there. [WIPING NOSE] Because there was only one rabbi. For three services were being held in a building which from the outside didn't look like a synagogue and therefore was not burnt. But inside there were three floors, and three different communities did their services there. And there was only one Orthodox rabbi, and he took turns one week, one Synagogue, and the next one was the next floor. So he spoke to me the following week. Food, to have a meal for bar mitzvah was impossible to obtain. But my father was able to order two chickens from Holland, and Holland still had meat available, Kosher meat. Because the Germans had not invaded Holland yet. He ordered two chickens to be sent to us in Frankfurt. And the ten men who assembled in our apartment had those two chickens, but there was not enough, unfortunately, for the ladies. And we had some vegetables, some half rotten vegetables, because the Jewish vegetable stores got the vegetables that were rejected by the non-Jewish vegetable stores. So by the time you came home and looked what was given to you, half of it had to be thrown away. But we made the best of it. And with some potatoes, that was my bar mitzvah meal. [PAUSES 5 SECONDS] [WIPING NOSE]

INT: Is there anything else you want to share with us about this time where you are still in Germany without your father? Any other memories?

NS: The memory that I have, as most strongly in my mind is the fear of a child, of being hurt, of being caught by the Nazis and being beaten up. And on the Sabbath and Jewish holidays when we had to walk to synagogue because, as I said before, we weren't able to use a bicycle, we always had to look ahead to see what was ahead of us, a block or two ahead, and if you saw any assembly of men or women or boys or girls, non-Jewish, we would stop and back up and go around the block. There was a constant fear that something would happen to us, or what happened to my mother, or what happened to our apartment that we lived in, and we had moved by that time from the apartment where we had lived during Kristallnacht to an apartment in a Jewish district. It was not a ghetto, but Jews moved together for economic reasons, and we were at that time four families in one apartment. All uncles, aunts and cousins. And none of them survived. All of them were transported to the concentration camp after we left, and they were all killed in concentration camp. It was a fear that continued even until we came to the United States. I remember in the United States, if I would see somebody with a uniform, be it a policeman or a soldier or Boy Scout or whatever it was, I would want to stop and back up and go around the block. But then I realized this was United States, this was not Germany anymore. But the fear was what was predominant in our mind all the time.

INT: When did you leave Germany?

NS: We left Germany...

I can tell you a story about how we left Germany before we left.

INT: Please.

NS: Jews had to report to the police every week. Why did they want to know? They wanted to know where you lived. So that when the time came to transport you off to the concentration camp, they didn't have to look for you. So all Jews had to report to the police once a week. [CLEARS THROAT] One day, when my mother was at the police, the officer tells her, if you still have money, I can get you airplane tickets to fly out of Germany. My mother realized that giving money to a policeman was bribery. And bribery in any country is prohibited. Certainly in Germany, it was a ticket for immediately transportation to the concentration camp. So she didn't say anything, she just said, "I'll be back next week." [CLEARS THROAT] She came home and she got together her family, her uncles and-- my uncles and aunts to talk about what to do. There was only two things you could do. Either you do or you don't. If you don't, that means you're sitting there waiting to be transported to concentration camp. So if she does, maybe it works, but if it doesn't, it couldn't be any worse than if she does nothing. So the decision was to take money and next week go to the police and give the police the money. And when that day came, we said goodbye to my mother because we did not know whether we would ever see her again. She came to the police and she handed the envelope to the policeman, knowing this was a time when she could be arrested but nothing happened. And the policeman said to her, "Come back next week and I'll have my tickets for you." And she turned around, knowing that the moment she left his room, that would be the time she could be arrested, because she had bribed a policeman. But she left the room, she left the building, and nothing happened, and she came home and we were happy to see her again. The following week she went back to the police and the policeman handed her an envelope with three Lufthansa tickets, the same Lufthansa that flies out of Germany today, to leave Germany on January 6th, 1941. And when that day came, we packed our few suitcases with a few things that we could take along and went by train to Munich, from where the plane was supposed to be leaving. And we come to Munich and we were told the airport is closed. There had been a snowstorm and no planes were leaving. It was not like today where they have these huge snowplows and they can clear a runway in a short time. This was 1941, and passenger planes flying was not very common yet. So we were standing there on the platform, not knowing what to do. We knew we couldn't go back to Frankfurt because we had told the police we're giving up our apartment. We couldn't go to a restaurant in Munich because the restaurant would have a sign, We couldn't go to a grocery to buy food because the stores had signs We couldn't even go to a bench in a park and sit down because every bench had a sign And as we were standing there and, you know, standing in Germany is always dangerous and not allowed, certainly not as Jews. A uniformed railroad officer comes over to us and says to my mother, "What are you doing here?" My mother says, "We just came from Frankfurt. We were supposed to take a plane out of the airport, but the airport is closed we don't know where to go. And the man says to her, "Go over to the platforms and there's a train leaving for Stuttgart, and all the planes that are supposed to be leaving from Munich are now leaving from Stuttgart." And my mother says to him, "I can't." "Why not?" "I have no more money. I used practically the last money that I had to buy the tickets to come here." "You don't need any tickets. Nobody would ask you for a railroad ticket. Nobody would ask you for money. Just go on the plane." Another thing that is unexplainable. When do you-- When can you go on a train and not pay for anything and not have a ticket? But that's exactly what happened. We went on the train, the train went to Stuttgart, and in Stuttgart we went to the airport and we went on a four-motor Lufthansa. And as we went on the plane, we saw who was on the plane. Uniformed Army, Air Force, Navy and civilians who we knew were spies going to the spy headquarters in Madrid. But the men and the mothers looked at each other and said, "What's going to happen to us when the door is closed? We are at their mercy, they can do with us whatever they want." And the fear that everybody had on all these days and weeks, and months, came to the forefront. Fear of what would happen. And the doors of the airplane closed and nobody looked at us. Nobody spoke to us, nobody even recognized us. Because after all, we were only Jews, less than human beings. We sat in our seats, and one of the men, as my mother told me later had said to her, "If there would be a hole in the floor, I would like to crawl into it because I am so afraid." But the plane went to LeÃ³n because there was no food on board. The Germans would not allow a plane to carry food out of Germany, and the plane took on food, but nothing was kosher, so we couldn't eat anything. And all I ate was ice cold grapes and I got very sick. And the plane flew to Barcelona. In Barcelona, the HIAS, Hebrew Immigrant Aid Society, took us from the plane and put us up in a hotel and gave us a hot meal to eat. Next morning, they picked us up again from the hotel, took us back to the airport to a two-motor Lufthansa and we flew to Madrid. And Madrid was supposed to be nonstop, but again, the restaurant was not kosher. So my mother went into the restaurant to see what was there, and she saw a pot boiling with rice. And she asked the man, the chef, "What's in that pot?" He said, it's just rice and water. So, that was our lunch. And after lunch, back to the airport and to another Lufthansa, two motor plane, we flew to Lisbon. In Lisbon, my mother had a distant relative where we stayed for ten days until we get a ship, the SS Siboney, the flagship, so we could go to the United States. But the danger wasn't over because the Germans had mined the harbors outside of Portugal, which was a neutral country, and the U-boats were there with torpedoes, and they didn't care who was on board the ship. They would torpedo us and we had to wear our life rafts, life vests from the first day on, always unless we were sleeping. And we left Lisbon, and I was airsick before we even left Lisbon harbor, and on board the ship, and I was seasick all the way to New York. And we had an Afro-American band who had been touring Germany on board the ship. And we did not have a private cabin, but it was a large room. And he-- I had a musician on top of me, and I had the bottom, and he said, he has a cure for seasickness. And I was so sick, I was willing to try everything. And I just told him, okay, and he went to the kitchen and got corn on the cob. Now, if you know, in Germany, corn on the cob is only food for pigs. It's not for human consumption. I had never seen corn on the cob. I didn't know what to do with it, or how to eat it, and he showed me. But it didn't help. I was still seasick all the time. [PAUSES] And we came to New York and my father was there at the pier. My uncle and aunt were there. They took us to their apartment. My first impression of the United States. I always had been interested in automobiles, so I looked at the long line of cars that were lined up there at the pier and they were all yellow. So my conclusion was that all cars in the United States are yellow. Not did I know that in New York all taxis are yellow. But I learned that very quickly. [PAUSES]

INT: I have a couple of questions for you. When did you arrive in the U.S?.

NS: I don't remember the exact date, but I think the trip took about ten days, so it must have been about January, 16th. more or less a couple of days, 1941. [PAUSES 5 SECONDS]

INT: You mentioned you took a couple of bags, what you could take with you. What did you take with you when you left Germany?

NS: We had boxes that we were allowed to ship to Lisbon weeks before. Once we knew that we had booked the passage on the ship out of Lisbon. But when we got to Lisbon and tried to collect the boxes, unfortunately, [CLEARS THROAT] my mother had no money anymore and they would not release the boxes because the warehouse charges had to be paid, transportation charges had to be paid, and my mother had no money for it. So we never got the content of these boxes. They were just auctioned off and we never got any proceeds. So what we took with us was just a couple of suitcases. We had some personal belongings, and each of us was able to take one particular item that we preferred. I had a stamp collection which was packed in the boxes, and I lost that stamp collection. [PAUSES 11 SECONDS]

INT: I wanted to ask you about something you said with regard to getting on the plane, with all the people in uniform and whatever--

NS: The plane.

INT: How would they know if you were Jewish or not?

NS: You could look at the Jewish person and could tell from the clothing and their behavior that they were Jewish. The Nazis had given out pictures of what the Jews looked like, so that not a non-Jew could identify the Jew. And Jews dressed differently, they behaved differently. You could see the fear in the Jews, and that gave us away. They knew-- They knew that we were Jews.

INT: Was there anything like a Star of David or a "J" in your passport or anything like that, that would be a giveaway.

NS: No. The Star of David was only given out six months later, or actually nine months later in September, 1941. So we had nothing to give us away as Jews, except they could tell they looked like Jews, behaved like Jews. [CLEARS THROAT] [PAUSES]

INT: Take a drink. Wait a minute.

NS: [DRINKING] [PAUSES 11 SECONDS]

INT: When you first came to the United States, what adjustments were difficult for you, for your parents, for your brother? Can you tell us what that experience was like for all of you?

NS: Naturally language was a problem for all four of us. Although both my brother and I had taken some English lessons for two years already in Frankfurt, in the Jewish school, in anticipation that we would probably end up in the United States. And the language was important. But my parents did not have that experience, so for them it was more difficult. And initially they spoke German at home, but then after a while they decided there's another way of learning English, but by speaking German. So they did as best as they could, spoke in English. Another adjustment they had to make is that the clothing that we wore, that we came from Germany, were typical German clothing that was not worn in the United States. So on the very first day that we were in New York, my aunt took us downtown to the east side and bought us jackets, and pants, and shirts that we would look like if we could-- At least we couldn't be identified as immigrants. So those were the two main problems. But after a couple of months, my parents were able to rent an apartment, and fortunately the public school was just a block away and we started in public school.

INT: How was that adjustment for you?

NS: Well, it was difficult and sometimes a comedy because when we would mispronounce something and the rest of the class would start laughing. I can give you one example. I was asked to read something from a book, and I did my best that I could because reading in English is easier than speaking English, and I came across a word that I did not know how to pronounce in English, so I pronounced it the way it would be pronounced in German. The word was E-U-C-L-I-D, euclid, but in German it's pronounced, And when I said And it didn't make me feel very comfortable. It's just-- But that's part of the learning process. [PAUSES 6 SECONDS]

INT: How were you treated by Americans when you got here?

NS: We were treated very well. I don't recall any acts of anti-Semitism during those years, or any comments from any of the students or the teachers. We were comfortable but it took us a long time to get rid of our fears that were ingrained in us. [PAUSES]

INT: You mentioned you didn't experience any anti-Semitism. Was there any anti-foreign sentiment? There's a war going on in Europe with Germany. And did anybody say anything about you being German?

NS: As a child, we were really not into following the war, but my parents did, and we heard my parents talk about it. We didn't understand everything they were saying, but obviously it was a problem. [WIPING NOSE] [PAUSES 6 SECONDS]

INT: After what you had gone through in Germany when you got here and you saw some of the racism here, what was your reaction to that?

NS: Reaction to what?

INT: Some of the racism that was in this country after what you went through in Germany.

NS: It did. We were not involved with... You're talking about racism? It was not something that as children we were involved with. So we were not-- We didn't hear anything, we didn't see anything. And we just simply assumed that there was no problem in the United States about being Jews. We walked on the street, except for the fact that we were afraid when you saw a uniform, we walked on the street without being afraid. We had everything that we needed. [PAUSES]

INT: What was going on in your life from 1941 until the end of the war in '45?

NS: We went through-- I went through the public school. I was put into 8A, a lower level of the eighth grade, although I should have been in the upper level, but because of my language problem they were But then I skipped the upper 8B and it was put right away into 9A and I skipped nine B, and I graduated from junior high school. And for reasons which I do not know, I was accepted in one of the best New York schools, Stuyvesant High School. [PAUSES] I took a test to get in. It was not a test in English. It was a test in science and math. And science and math had been major efforts in Frankfurt. And I was better in math and science than the average American was. And maybe that's the reason why they accepted me. And I went through Stuyvesant High School in two and a half years. I graduated from high school in 1944. [UNCLEAR] I came to the United States only in 1941 and a year in junior high school. So high school went very fast. [WIPING NOSE] I had always been interested in automobiles and always wanted to be an automobile engineer designing cars. So after graduating from high school, I went to the City College of New York Engineering School. Although everybody told me, "Don't go to engineering school. Engineers are very [CLEARS THROAT] anti-Semitic, and as a Jew you would have problems." But I was in the United States, I was not afraid of being a Jew. And I started going to engineering school. [COUGHING] But I knew that I was 17 years old. And at 18, with the war still on, I would be drafted. So my efforts in school was not very great, and my grades were not very great either. My brother was already in the army because he was a year older and since the war in Europe was already over, they did not-- The Army did not want to have two brothers in the army at the same time. So I got a six months deferment, when I was 18, in March 1945, they gave me six months deferment until September 1945, and I was drafted in September 1945. And it is a family joke as to what happened in September 1945. September 1945 was also when the Japanese surrendered. And the joke is, "Why did the Japanese surrender?" "They knew that Norbert Strauss was coming into the army and they surrendered." So that's funny. And I got my basic training in Fort Dix. And the first day that I was in Fort Dix, I had KP duty working in the kitchen, cleaning pots in which cheese and spaghetti had been cooked, and after smelling the cheese and spaghetti for 24 hours, I could not stand the smell anymore. And for years afterwards, I could not walk past a store where cheese was being cooked.

But I survived that. And a couple of weeks into my basic training, I was called in to my captain, the company commander, and I was wondering, "What did I do wrong? Why was I being called in?" And he said to me, "Based on your record, when you finish with the basic training, you will be assigned to West Point as an instructor." I was so shocked, I didn't ask any questions. I didn't ask, "Instructor in what?" I just said okay and I turned around and walked back to my bunk. To this day, I don't know what I was being instructed because it never happened. A week later, he called me in again and he said, "Strauss, you cannot go to West Point because you're not a citizen yet." Although a few weeks later, I was taken from the field in my field uniform and taken to the capital in Alabama, I had my training in Alabama.

And I was made a citizen. And despite whatever, today takes tests and problems, my whole citizenship application took about half an hour. And it just said, "Now you are an American citizen. Raise your hand." And now I was a citizen, but the West Point never came back. When I was finished with my basic training, it was in Anniston, Alabama.

I had furlough for two weeks, and then I was reassigned to report to Army camp in Georgia for reassignment. And there something very strange happened. There are three ways of doing things. There's a right way, a wrong way, and an army way. Well, what happened to me in the camp there was the army way. I'll explain. I was bunked together with a fellow from Seattle, who was a typical American boy, very nice boy. But he spoke no languages. He knew no languages. And he was being assigned to interpreter school to teach him German, to send him to Germany, to be an interpreter. And I, who spoke, read and wrote German fluently, I was sent to the Pacific to Korea.

And I ended up in California and put on a ship. And the typical American boy had no idea of where Korea was. In fact, the name had been changed. The Japanese who had occupied Korea, their name was Chosen, C-H-O-S-E-N. And I, who knew geography very well because geography was one of my preferences,

I was able to explain to them where Korea was. And that they were the Japanese occupied country during the war, and that now the Americans had taken it over. And we came to Korea, we landed in Incheon. Incheon has a very flat harbor, and the ship could not even go to a pier. We had to climb down the sides of the ship into barges that went onto the pier with our packs. And I was in the infantry. And during infantry, we do guard duty. Guard duty is not very challenging. It's two hours on, four hours off, two hours on, four hours off. Day and night.

And something happened to get me out of the infantry. My mother and another lady who stood next to her in the synagogue at [?Breuer's] in Washington Heights, they talked about their sons. My mother said, "My son just got to Korea, and he's in the infantry, and he's not happy there." And the other lady, whose name was Kissinger, said to my mother, "My son is also in Korea. He was already discharged from the army and he has the highest civilian post. Why doesn't your son contact my son? And maybe he can help get him out of the infantry?" And so my mother wrote to me and I contacted Walter Kissinger, the younger brother of Henry Kissinger, who in years later would become our Secretary of State.

And he asked me to come to his office. He said, "Well, no problem, we'll handle it. Just make an application to your company commander and request a transfer to the military government where he worked." And so I did, I went to my company commander, and he said to me, "Strauss, you can apply, but I know it's going to be rejected. I will accept your application but the battalion commander is not allowing anybody to leave his battalion because he is short staffed." And so I applied and he passed it on. And as he said, it was rejected. So I went back to Kissinger and said it was rejected. So he said, okay, he will apply through Corps headquarters, which is about as high as you can go to request for a transfer. And so he applied, and Major despite the fact that the application had come through Corps headquarters. So I went back to Kissinger, and said to him, "It doesn't go up, it doesn't go down. What do we do next?" And he says, "I don't know, something will come up, don't worry." [PAUSES] And something did come up. One day I was standing at night on guard duty, and the lieutenant drives up and says, "Strauss, come into the jeep. You're being reassigned." I knew nothing. I drove back to my bunk, packed my belongings. And next morning, I reported to Kissinger in the military government in the main capital building in Seoul, Korea. And he told me what happened. And if he didn't show me the documents, the story is unbelievable. The story is that Major [?Bong?] came to Kissinger, and Kissinger's responsibility was to approve any work that the Army wanted to have done from the industry in Korea, and any work which the industry in Korea wanted to have done by the army. It had to go through him and he had to prove it. So one day Major [?Bong?] came to him and Major [?Bong?] did not know who Kissinger was. But Kissinger right away recognized the name Major who had twice rejected the request for transfer. And Major [?Bong?] asked Kissinger whether the Korean civilian aluminum industry could make the mess trays for the Army, who were still eating out of field kits because MacArthur kept everything in Japan.

And Kissinger said, "Yes, sure they can. But I have one request from you." And Major [?Bong?] said, "Sure, anything you want." And Kissinger said, "I want one of your men in your battalion to be transferred to the military government." "Sure. No problem." And that's how I got out of the infantry into the military government. And the following day I reported to Kissinger in his office, and he assigned me to the lowest level in his office to run a mimeograph machine, the old type with a purple drum, that after the day was over, your face was purple, your hands were purple, your clothing was purple. But that was my first job, and I was out of the infantry.

And I was in the military government for about 13 months. And he, Kissinger, got me a promotion from private first class to corporal. And a few months later he got me promotion from corporal to sergeant. And a further promotion to staff sergeant was in the works, but my papers were already on their way back to the United States for reassignment. But I had two titles during my job. From running a mimeograph machine, I ended up as chief clerk of the Department of Commerce and sergeant major of the Department of Commerce. Chief clerk was in charge of all the civilian personnel.

And sergeant major was in charge of all the non-commissioned army personnel. And when I was asked what title I wanted because I could not have two titles on my discharge papers, Sergeant major didn't sound right to me because it sounded like military title. It wasn't a military title, it was a position. So I said, "I'll take chief clerk." So my discharge papers called me chief clerk, and I was discharged. And I came back to the United States, and back to Fort Dix and was discharged, and I came home. And I since I had worked in an office, I had changed my mind about becoming an engineer. I went to Bernard Baruch Business School to work in an office, because I had fallen in love with office work. [PAUSES 8 SECONDS]

INT: I have a few questions about-- I'm going to go back chronologically, so I want to go back and cover this. [PAUSES] During the war, still during World War II, what did you know during the war about what was happening to the Jews under Hitler?

NS: Even when still in Frankfurt, we knew that Jews were being transported off to Poland. We did not know of concentration camps, we knew of labor camps. But how did we know? Because already Jews who had come into Germany, ten, twenty years earlier, for economic reasons or maybe for military reasons, already had disappeared. I will come to school in the morning, and a boy who the day before was sitting next to me was gone. Or maybe even my teacher was gone because they were Ethiopian Jews and they had been transported out and many of them just dumped at the Polish border. So we knew there were camps, but they were known as labor camps. Details of concentration camps Auschwitz and Birkenau were not known at the time to the public. [PAUSES 6 SECONDS]

INT: When did you find out about the extermination camps? How did that news reach you?

NS: I think that it was sometime after the war's end, when the American troops and the Russian troops, and the French and the British troops entered Germany and saw the concentration camps and saw the skeletons and saw human skeletons, live skeletons and dead skeletons, that's when the information became available.

INT: You mentioned quite a while ago that your family members that were left behind did not survive. What do you know about their fates?

NS: I know they were transported to Minsk. At which camp exactly they were killed? I don't know. But they all ended up except one cousin of mine, for whom there was no record as to where he was transported to. But he was also killed because he never reappeared. [PAUSES]

INT: Do you remember your parents making any efforts to find family members after the war?

NS: Yes. The Jewish organizations, both in the United States and Israel, were making lists of camp survivors. And these lists slowly became available. And you could look over the list to see whether any familiar names were there. But we never found any of my family. [PAUSES 7 SECONDS]

INT: What anti-Semitism or anti-foreign sentiments did you experience in the Army, if any?

NS: There were some comments of fellows near my bunk. Fellows who had never met a Jew before and made derogatory statements but there was never anything physical. And they were very careful not to have a superior hear them say that, because it was forbidden to make any racial comments. [PAUSES 5 SECONDS]

INT: Did you follow the Nuremberg trial at all?

NS: Yes, we did, whatever we read in the newspaper. [PAUSES 5 SECONDS]

INT: Do you feel justice was done?

NS: I always feel that prison for life is more of a punishment than being killed for what you did. But that was the decision of the judges as to how they should be punished. Unfortunately, many of them were not killed, but they committed suicide. It would have been better if they would have been killed. But they came ahead and had the cyanide in their mouths or whatever they did and committed suicide. But it's all the same as long as they were dead. I personally feel that prison for life is more of a punishment. [PAUSES 6 SECONDS]

INT: What do you remember about the UN debate for the partition of Palestine and the birth of the State of Israel? How closely did you follow it and what did that mean to you?

NS: Again, what we read in the paper. We saw what was happening, the fighting between the Arabs and the Jews and the Jews and the British. And the day came when the British left Palestine and the Jews declared Israel independent as a country. But again, we saw in the paper the fighting and the killings that happened. And we were very concerned about it, because my mother had her mother still living in Palestine and two sisters with families in Palestine, and naturally we were concerned about their safety. [PAUSES 5 SECONDS]

INT: Were they in Palestine during World War II?

NS: No, they had moved to Palestine before the war. Before Kristallnacht, in fact. [PAUSES 6 SECONDS]

INT: Have you been to Israel?

NS: Have I been to Israel? I've made it a point since the early 1950s to go to Israel at least once every year. And I was successful, in some years even going twice, because I was very active as a volunteer with the Shaare Zedek Hospital in Jerusalem. And after a time, I became the director of not only the American Committee for the hospital here in New York, but also on the board of directors of the hospital itself and had to go to board meetings. And also the company that I worked for had some business connections in Palestine-- in Israel. And for a couple of years I went three times to Israel. But I try to make it a point of going to Israel at least once every year.

INT: Can you tell us about this work that you did, this volunteer position?

NS: I worked for a company by the name of Philipp Brothers. After I graduated from from college, I applied to two companies. One was a company that my father did business with, and they interviewed me for a traffic position because I had taken majority traffic courses in City College. And they said, "Yes, I think we're interested in you. We'll let you know tomorrow." That same day in the afternoon, I had another interview with a company by the name of Philipp Brothers, where a friend of my mother from Hamburg, where she was born, was the executive secretary of the chairman of the board. and she got me the interview. And I got an interview. And if you compare it with what interviews are now with the investigations of FBI, and CIA, and police, and all kinds of investigations that before you get hired, your paper file is an inch high. But I was interviewed, and the chairman of the board was sitting behind his desk, and the head of the traffic department was sitting on one end of the table, I was sitting at the other end of the table, and after a few questions of my background, the chairman of the board, whose name was Siegfried Ullman, turned to the head of the traffic department name of and said, "I think we can use him in your department." And I was hired, that was it. I started as a traffic clerk, doing calculations all day long, because part of the work of a traffic clerk was calculating and issuing invoices to customers. And I was doing the calculations, some very complicated calculations, but all multiplications, divisions, percentages, additions too. But math was not a problem for me, I was quite comfortable with math. But my "office" in quotes consisted of a small table in the office of the head of the department, which was not very comfortable because he could always look over my shoulder. And one day he comes in the office and sits next to me, puts his hands on the papers that I was working on and says, what is the name of the cargo that you're working on? I had no idea because the name of the ship meant nothing to me. It's the name of the commodity and how much and so on. I said, "I don't know." "Well, let me teach you a lesson. Every cargo is identified by the name of the ship that the cargo came on or the name of the ship So you have to know the name of the ship in order to identify a cargo." From that point on, I knew always the name of the ship that I was working on. And from that position in his office, I became a traffic clerk. And I worked hard to do the right thing. And when the time came for the traffic manager, Mr. Fishman, to retire, he appointed three people to be at Traffic managers. There was a Mr. Stern, who had been hired a few years earlier, there was a Mr. Larry Cohen, who had been hired about eight years earlier. And they both had seniority over me. But we tried to work together. But eventually the point came where I realized, you can't work as a committee, you cannot run a department by a committee. And I went to Ludwig Jesselson, who was the president at the time, and told him, "Mr. Jesselson, you have to appoint a traffic manager. It doesn't work as a committee. I have all the responsibility and the others get all the credit because they have more seniority. Please appoint one of us three, and it's your choice, as a traffic manager. And the others will be assistant traffic managers." And he said to me, "I cannot appoint you because the others have seniority, but you go take it."

And I said, "What do you mean, 'take it'?" "You go act as traffic manager, and the others will, after a while accept you because you are the better man." And so I went back to my desk and I told the other two who had seniority, what I had been told, and I never tried to step on their feet. I tried to work together with them, always consulting with them, whatever had to be done. And the time came when I got a promotion to general traffic manager and I asked Mr. Jesselson to promote the others to traffic manager so that they would only be one step behind me. And when, after 36 years, I saw the handwriting on the wall, I took early retirement as General Traffic Manager, and by that time I had also become a group vice president of the company. And the others, I pulled them along, they became vice presidents because I wanted them always to be just one step behind me. And I had worked for Philipp Brothers for 36 years, and I took early retirement because I saw the handwriting on the wall that the need for middlemen and Philipp Brothers was a company that acted as a middleman between producers and consumers in the metal and oil field. I took early-- [PAUSES]

NS: Yes?

INT: I'm sorry. You had already told us you took early retirement. I had asked you about your work. You said you did volunteer work with a hospital in Jerusalem.

NS: That was--

INT: I was wondering, where does that fit in?

NS: That was volunteer work, which started as I was working for Philipp Brothers and continued for many years thereafter. I was active as a volunteer for a little over 30 years, both in the American Committee for Shaare Zedek Hospital and at the same time also as board of directors of the hospital itself, and then eventually also as a board of governors. [PAUSES]

INT: When you and I spoke earlier, you had mentioned that you also worked with the Israeli Army. Can you tell us about that?

NS: I'm sorry, I didn't understand your question.

INT: When you and I spoke previously, you had mentioned that you worked with the Israeli Army. Can you tell us about that?

NS: In 1982, I was on vacation at a hotel. It happened to be the hotel where I met my first wife. I was there with my first wife. And at the hotel, they had two guests. Two Israeli Army officers who talked to the people who were at the hotel asking for volunteers because the Lebanese war had started, the first Lebanese war. And soldiers, many of them had come from the yeshivas in Golan Heights. And they needed volunteers, both for agricultural and military work, to take the place of the soldiers who had gone into the armed forces and they asked for volunteers. And I asked my wife, how she felt about it, and she said, "Go." And I went to my boss and said, "Can I have four weeks off?" He said, "Go." Everybody agreed, so I applied. And I was promptly rejected. I was too old, I was 55 years old. And they were looking for 18 years old, 19, not 55-year-old men. But I don't take no for an answer. So I applied again, and I was again rejected. And I still don't take no for an answer and I applied for the third time. And finally they said, "If you are accepted by your own doctor, after you get an examination, you can go to Israel and be examined by an army doctor, and if he passes you, we'll take you in." And I was passed by my doctor, and I went to Israel, and I was passed by the army doctor, and I was given the choice of either doing agricultural work or going into the army. And since I had been in the American Army, and I knew which way the rifle-- the bullet came out of, I chose military work. For the first week, I helped the army officers teach the others who had never held a rifle, never held a pistol, never thrown a hand grenade, never shot a machine gun. And then I was assigned to the Border Patrol on Golan Heights, facing the Syrian Army. There was problems because that particular post were five or six Israeli soldiers, none of them Orthodox. And the food in the Israeli Army is only kosher up to the tailgate of the truck that brings the food once it leaves the tailgate and goes to the kitchen, if the kitchen isn't kosher, and the kitchen there was not kosher, it was in a tunnel dug into a hill. So I had to be there when the food truck came and I got myself food from the tailgate for myself so that I could have something to eat. But it was a very interesting and challenging job, because I would stand in a trench and look at the Syrian soldiers looking at me. I had a Zeiss binocular, one of these big binoculars that are used to look at these soldiers. And I saw the Syrian soldiers look at me using its highest binocular, probably the same type as I was using. I was only warned never stretch out your hand because from the Syrian trenches, a stretched hand might look like a rifle, and they might think that somebody is pointing a rifle at them. And when the time came for the post to be deactivated, I helped the soldiers deactivate, loading the hand grenades and the artillery shells onto a truck. Being very careful when picking up a case of hand grenades that there were no scorpions underneath the case because they are not very pleasant animals. [PAUSES 7 SECONDS]

INT: Thank you for sharing that.

You married? You have a family?

What do you tell people, your family and others, about your childhood experiences?

NS: What do I tell my family about the childhood experiences? I was urged by my grandson and my son, Benjamin, when he was still alive, he unfortunately died in 2004, to put in writing my experience, because when I'm gone, nobody would know anymore what happened to me and the story that I have and that many other people have is very important to be able to remember the Holocaust. And they urged me to put it in writing. And I started writing first my Holocaust story, and then I added other parts. And finally it ended up in a book called My Stories. And I gave copies to my children, and to my grandchildren, and to my great-grandchildren, urged everybody to read the book, to talk about the book, and to pass on the story of the Holocaust.

And that's how they learned about what happened to me.

INT: Is that the book that you referred to earlier, or are there other books?

NS: I will show it later.

