INT: Have you gone back to Germany since the war?

NS: Yes, I went back to Germany. The first time it was very difficult because I had never wanted to go back to Germany. But when my company assigned me to the Amsterdam office for four weeks, my father said to me, "Go to my birthplace and see what condition the Jewish cemetery is in." And although I didn't want to go, when your father asked you something, you do it. So I went to Germany with my wife. But I took the opportunity to go first to Frankfurt to take a look at the buildings where we had lived. And one of the buildings was not standing anymore. Nobody could tell me whether they had torn it down or whether a bomb had hit it. Because while we were still in Frankfurt, the British army was bombing the industrial suburbs of Frankfurt. And bombs were less exact then as they are now, and bombs sometimes hit residential buildings. So I looked at these buildings and took photographs, and then went to my birthplace, Bad Homburg, and did the same thing there. Two buildings that I had lived in, and took photographs and then drove with my Volkswagen to my father's hometown, where he had been born, where my uncle had had his restaurant, and looked at the cemetery and everything was in beautiful condition. Why? Because a Protestant minister had taken as his responsibility to take care of not only the Jewish cemetery in Schmitten, but the Jewish cemeteries in a number of other villages in the neighborhood. He made sure that the fence stayed in good condition, that no branches overhang the fences, that the grass was cut, that all the cemetery stones were standing and nothing fell over. And I was able to report to my father that the cemetery was in excellent condition. [CLEARS THROAT] Subsequently I went to Germany because I received an invitation from the city of Frankfurt for a two week, all expense paid trip for my wife and myself. I knew that sooner or later I would get that invitation and I would throw it back at them saying, "I don't want to come." I don't want to go to Germany. But others who had had the same invitation urged me to say yes, when I get the invitation. And when I finally got the invitation, [PAUSES] I didn't know what to do. So I asked my rabbi, Rabbi Menachem Genack, and told him what my problem was. And he said, "Norbert, if you can talk to the college students about your Holocaust experience, then it is worthwhile going because hearing from you as a personal experience is worth a thousand words in a book." So I went. And the two weeks I did not participate in all the activities because I wanted to speak in schools, and I did speak four times in three different cities. One village that I spoke in had never had any Jewish residents, but believe it or not, they had a matzah factory before the war, before the Nazi time, and they had never heard any Jew come and speak to them, so I spoke to them. And I spoke in Homburg, where I was born, in the school where I went to school, and I spoke in two sites in Frankfurt. One of them was in a school that was adjoining the [UNCLEAR] that I went to. And I told the students, if you could look through that wall, you could see the seat that I occupied before I was thrown out of Germany. [WIPING NOSE]

I spoke three times in English because the students had enough English lessons to be able to understand. And I told them if you want to ask me questions afterwards in German, I will answer you. But the fourth time there were younger students, they did not have enough English, and I was asked whether I could speak in German. And I said to the managers, I don't know whether I can, but I will try. Perhaps their teacher, their English teacher present told, if I can't think of the right word, he can help me with it. And it went quite well. I spoke in German. One thing very interesting that I heard almost in all schools. They asked the question. Students asked the question. After I finished speaking, they said, "Do you blame us for what happened?" [COUGHING] [PAUSES] [DRINKING]

INT: Are you okay or would you like to take a break?

NS: Pardon me?

INT: Are you okay or do you want--

NS: I'm fine. Just my throat, it gets a little dry.

INT: Okay.

NS: "Do you blame us for what happened?" And I said, "How can I blame you? You were not even alive." The next question would be, "Do you blame our parents?" Well, the chances were that their parents were maybe members of the Hitler Youth, but certainly not SS or SA.

But the third question was a difficult one. "Do you blame our grandparents?" I did not want to say, "Yes," because there were Germans who were not Nazis, who joined the Nazis because they had to. And maybe the grandparent of that student who was asking the question was not a Nazi. So I thought about it for a minute and I said, "I cannot be the judge of who is guilty. The judge has to be God." And they were satisfied with that answer. And I had the same, almost same question in every school. [PAUSES] One more time I went to Germany with my daughter and my wife, Dorothy, because they had heard me tell my Holocaust story so many times. And Dorothy is always present, since we were married, every time that I speak, and many times my daughter Esther is present.

They had never been to Germany. They had never been to Europe. Although my wife is born in Paris, but she was just a couple of weeks old-- and they wanted to see what I was talking about. So I went with them. We went to Germany, we went to Frankfurt. We went to Homburg, we went to Schmitten. We visited all the places that I had been talking about. And since then I have been in Germany a number of times on business. But I never wanted to stay [WIPING NOSE] overnight in Germany. If I had to be in the northern part of Germany, I would stay in our Amsterdam office, go to work during the day to Germany and go back at night to Amsterdam. If I had to be in the southern part of Germany, I would go to a Swiss office and stay there overnight. Go to Germany during the day and go back to Switzerland at night. It was just a feeling, I did not want to be comfortable in Germany. [PAUSES 8 SECONDS]

INT: You recently did a holographic interview. Can you share with us how that happened and what that experience was like? I'm a member of the speakers bureau of the Museum of Jewish Heritage, and as such, I was asked whether I wanted to participate. And I said yes, because I am willing to participate in anything that I can do to promote and to spread the word of the Holocaust. And it was a very interesting experience. I had-- It was together with my wife a couple of years ago, seeing a hologram in Florida where they had one. It was interesting because it really looked like the person that we were questioning was life. You could not really tell that it was just a hologram. So I participated and it was a very interesting experience. I haven't seen myself yet because it will be available only in November. But I look forward to seeing myself acting in a hologram. [PAUSES 6 SECONDS]

INT: How do you think World War II impacted your life? Including your career choice, your personal ambitions, anything. How did your wartime experiences impact your life?

NS: I think it impacted my life by wanting to spread the word of the Holocaust. And it impacted my life by making me realize the importance of honesty in relations to other people. How we had been treated by the Nazis was not how I want to treat anybody else. I wanted to treat other people the way I wanted to be treated. And I think I've done a good job. My father always used an expression, a Hebrew expression, [HEBREW] which means good name. The importance of keeping, having a good name. My father always did that, and I learned from him. And I tried my best that in all my activities, to be honest, to be straightforward, to be nice, and to develop a good name for myself and my family. [PAUSES 5 SECONDS]

INT: Did your wartime experiences impact your faith, your belief in Judaism?

NS: No, because we were Orthodox. My parents were Orthodox. My grandparents were Orthodox. As far back as I'm able to recollect from history. And there are stories about Orthodoxy. One story in my mother's family, my mother's father was known in Hamburg as [GERMAN] Nathan. His name was Nathan, Nathan in German. And [GERMAN] means engraver. He was making the signs on the doors in brass where people went-- They called themselves goldsmith. That means they had a gold sign on the door, or a [GERMAN] that's a red sign. And he made these signs, and he made the signet rings that people signed documents with.

And he was in business with two brothers. And the two brothers decided at one point that they move their business out of Hamburg to Altona, which was a sister company to Hamburg. But when they got to Altona, they decided they had to keep their business open on the Sabbath.

The two brothers had no problem with it. But my grandmother, my grandfather said, "No, I will not work with you if business open." And he went back to Hamburg. He got no money for his part in the business. He was so poor, to support his family, the synagogue had to give him support. And that shows how strong his belief was in Orthodoxy. And the same was in my father's business because my father's father, who had opened up the hotel and the restaurant in Schmitten, he slaughtered his own calves and sheep under supervision of the rabbinate of Frankfurt, and they allowed him to do it without having a supervisor present because of his reputation. So I know that Orthodoxy is in the family. [CLEARS THROAT] And Orthodoxy is in my family. My children were Orthodox. My grandchildren are Orthodox. My grandchildren are Orthodox. My great-grandchildren are Orthodox. And I hope when they grow up, my great-great-grandchildren will be Orthodox. [PAUSES 11 SECONDS]

INT: I wanted to ask you your thoughts on the situation in Israel today, since October 7th. And also the increase in anti-Semitism in the U.S. since that same time, since October. How do you reflect on what you've been seeing?

NS: I've been asked this question a number of times. In fact, practically, every time I speak about the Holocaust. One of the questioners always comes up with the question, "Can you comment on anti-Semitism as it is now in Europe and the United States, as against Hitler?" And I always give the same answer. There's a big difference between anti-Semitism then and anti-Semitism now. Anti-semitism then was instigated, and supported, and carried out by the government of Germany. And now in the United States, it is not the government, the opposite, the government in United States and in Europe is fighting anti-Semitism. It is the stupid people who don't even know what anti-Semitism very often is that are anti-Semitic. And I think that once the war is over and things get back to normal, I think that the danger of anti-Semitism in Europe and the United States will also calm down. [PAUSES 7 SECONDS]

INT: Is there anything we have not talked about that you would like to share before we have our final question?

NS: Would you give me a moment to look at the list that I had made?

INT: You want to take a break for a minute? We can take.

NS: Yes, take a break for a minute.

INT: Okay. Okay. We're back. And we had talked a while ago, and I didn't get a chance to ask you today. But now I have an opportunity.

How did your parents support themselves when they came to the United States during the war? What did your father do for a living, for example?

NS: When my father came to the United States first he got a job with a distant relative of my mother as a furniture finisher, and salesman in a used furniture store in Washington Heights. But when we came, and he had to support a family, the income from that job was not sufficient. He tried his best to try to start a business, the same business that he had in Frankfurt, wholesale hides and skins. But in order to start a business, you need money.

And during that time when he was not making enough money, my mother was working as a cleaning woman in other people's houses, and when that did not work out anymore, we did work at home. I remember working with my mother or my brother also, of making artificial paper flowers at home and giving them to the suppliers. Another time we were making industrial cufflinks, which you had to do with a pair of pliers. So my mother tried to help my father in trying to make a living, until eventually he met a man who had money. And also wanted to start a business, but did not have enough connections. So they became partners and they started a hides and skins business in a small way, using the money of the partner, because my father had no money. Unfortunately, after a year or two, I don't know what period of time, this partner committed suicide. Not because of anything to do with the business or my father, it was a personal matter and naturally the family pulled all the money that the man had put into the business, pulled the money out. So my father was back to square one. He had to now run the business without having any capital, and he ran the business on a very small scale, slowly building it up as he made money.

And slowly the business grew. My mother helped my father with the bookkeeping and the issuing of invoices and the paying of suppliers. Obviously, regular office work. And my brother and I worked in my father's warehouse. He had rented a three floor warehouse, not three floors up, but three floors down. Ground floor, basement and subbasement. Because the hides and the skins had to be treated before they could be sold to a tannery. And that was our job. My father would go out in the truck and buy skins and bring them to the warehouse. And my brother and I would work in the sub basement, which was rat infested, because there was a lot of food for the rats to eat, and the skins were pieces of fat and meat and so on. [CLEARS THROAT] We worked whenever we could.

In high school I was in the morning session, so I had the afternoon free to work for my father and in college, the irregular hours, they were here and there, a couple of hours that was free, I would always go to the warehouse and work. [COUGHING] [PAUSES 6 SECONDS] [DRINKING]

Sometimes the work was very difficult because one item that had to be put on the skins to treat them was salt. [COUGHING] And the salt was delivered in 80-pound bags and a truckload of salt. And I remember one time when my father had bought a railroad car full of salt, the biggest amount he ever bought, and it was being delivered while my brother was at school. And my father was out in the city buying more skins. And I was there by myself when the truck came to deliver the salt bags.

There was a hole in the floor of the ground floor through which the bags could be dropped, and the skins also would be dropped down to the sub basement. And I was there when they dropped the salt down. And slowly the pile of salt grew higher and higher, until the pile was so high that it reached the ceiling. And I was on the bottom of the pile, pulling the bags away so that they could finish dropping more bags on.

I was a teenager, and teenagers have unlimited strength. And I worked and I worked. And when my father came back home, when they were finished delivering, he asked me, "Who helped you with this?" I said, "Nobody, there's nobody here to help." "You did this all by yourself?" He couldn't believe it. And there were times when I wanted to change things that my father had taught me to do. Because my father had worked in this business for many years in Germany. And he knew every piece of business, how it was done, how the skins would be cleaned, how the head would be cut off, and so on. And he expected his sons to follow his steps. And the packing after the treatment had to be-- The skins had to be packed up into bundles, and I figured out a way of doing things easier, but my father would not accept it. So I did it my way whenever my father was not in the warehouse. And he always asked me, "How come you're finished already?" And I said, "Because I did it my way." Nothing to do with Frank Sinatra.

And he couldn't believe it. And finally he accepted the way I did it, because when he saw the result that I could do it much faster than he could do it. While my brother-- My brother, was a different type than me. He never had the initiative of changing. He did it exactly the way our father had taught him. So we helped my father until the time came [CLEARS THROAT] when the Department of Health of New York prohibited skins and hides to come into New York for sanitary reasons. So my father saw the business was dying, and he sold his business and retired. And retiring for him meant working as a volunteer for the synagogue and the Yeshiva. And he got good honors for the volunteer work that he did. [PAUSES 7 SECONDS]

